{"chunk_id":"R1-2601843-001","collection":"ran1_tdoc_chunks","metadata":{"source":["Nokia"],"tdocNumber":"R1-2601843","title":"Views on HARQ related aspects in 6GR"},"point_id":"10616750755305177981"}
rce allocation for the PUCCH on the uplink serving cell where the HARQ-ACK codebook would be received, and at the time of the PUCCH resource assignment the UL scheduler needs to know the size of the HARQ-ACK codebook corresponding to the DL assignment. In short, NR HARQ-ACK feedback requires that the PUCCH UL cell scheduling is coordinated with DL serving cell scheduling and, in the case of Type-2 HARQ-ACK codebook, DCI contents are aligned over DL assignments and UL grants of CA serving cells. Additionally, when UCI is multiplexed on PUSCH overlapping with PUCCH (within a PUCCH cell group), scheduling decisions on different UL serving cells can determine the cell on which HARQ-ACK feedback is transmitted. Above considerations for multiple cells on scheduling/timing coordination between DL and UL for NR HARQ-ACK operation also apply to a single cell scenario. Observation 1: For NR HARQ operation, the UL scheduler for the serving cell where HARQ-ACK feedback is received needs to coordinate with the DL schedulers for all the DL serving cells where the PDSCHs are transmitted. Observation 2: For NR Type-2 HARQ-ACK, in the case of self-scheduling of DL serving cells, the DAI information on a DCI scheduling PDSCH on the serving cell depends on the simultaneous scheduling decisions on other DL cells, which further increases the scheduling coordination between the DL serving cells. Observation 3: For NR Type-2 HARQ-ACK, when the HARQ-ACK information is to be mapped on PUSCH, the final DAI information of the Type-2 HARQ-ACK codebook needs to be included in the DCI format scheduling the PUSCH, which further increases the scheduling/timing coordination between DL and UL. It would be beneficial for 6GR to enhance the NR HARQ-ACK operation so that the HARQ-ACK reporting would not require a tight coordination between UL and DL schedulers, for single and multi-cell scenarios. Specifically, the triggering of the HARQ-ACK codebook report that includes HARQ-ACK feedback corresponding to the PDSCHs and the scheduling of the PDSCHs should be done in a more asynchronous way in the sense of reduced coordination between UL and DL schedulers. This would simplify the implementation, and in case of multiple cells, would allow more parallel processing of different CA serving cells. For multiple cells, DL scheduling on a DL serving cell would not require simultaneous UL scheduling decision for the PUSCH or PUCCH carrying the HARQ-ACK codebook on a UL serving cell. Such HARQ operation with reduced coordination between UL and DL schedulers is particularly important for a 6G CA framework where UL and DL carriers can be decoupled and UEs can be easily and quickly assigned UL and DL carriers depending on different deployments, spectrum availability, traffic conditions, etc. Support of a such flexible CA framework is of fundamental importance to ensure that CA would be widely deployed in 6G, and the introduction of mechanisms to reduce the coordination between UL and DL schedulers for the HARQ operation would facilitate the CA operation with decoupled UL and DL carriers. Figure 1 illustrates the NR CA design that requires several points of coordination between UL and DL schedulers, and a desired 6GR CA design where HARQ-ACK reporting is decoupled from DL scheduling. Observation 4: HARQ-ACK reporting with decoupled UL and DL scheduling simplifies the implementation and, in case of multiple cells, allows more parallel processing of different CA serving cells. Observation 5: For a 6G flexible CA framework where UL and DL carriers are decoupled and UEs can be more efficiently assigned UL and DL carriers depending on different deployments, HARQ-ACK reporting with decoupled UL and DL scheduling is needed.

